Fix a version-string bug in ext/curl's "old openssl" probe that makes the
legacy era link two different OpenSSLs into one process.

ext/curl/config.m4's AC_RUN_IFELSE probe reads curl_version_info()->ssl_version
and only recognizes the literal prefix "OpenSSL/1.1" as a "new" OpenSSL.
Trixie's system libcurl reports "OpenSSL/3.5.6", which starts with "OpenSSL"
but not "OpenSSL/1.1", so the probe falls through to its old-OpenSSL branch.
The probe predates OpenSSL 3 and never anticipated a version string that is
neither "1.1" nor actually old.

WHAT THE OLD BRANCH DOES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT DO

That branch does two things. Only the second one matters here:

  AC_DEFINE([HAVE_CURL_OLD_OPENSSL], ...)          <- inert in this build
  PKG_CHECK_MODULES([OPENSSL], [openssl], [
    PHP_EVAL_LIBLINE($OPENSSL_LIBS, CURL_SHARED_LIBADD)   <- this is the bug
    PHP_EVAL_INCLINE($OPENSSL_CFLAGS)
    AC_CHECK_HEADERS([openssl/crypto.h])
  ], [])

HAVE_CURL_OLD_OPENSSL has exactly one consumer in php-src -- interface.c's
`#if defined(ZTS) && defined(HAVE_CURL_OLD_OPENSSL)` locking shim (checked in
both 7.4.33 and 8.0.30: one AC_DEFINE, one guarded use). These images are NTS
("Thread Safety => disabled"), so that code is compiled out and the macro on
its own changes nothing.

The damage is PHP_EVAL_LIBLINE: it appends OpenSSL to ext/curl's link line. On
the legacy era php/build.sh puts the vendored prefix first in
PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR, so `openssl.pc` resolves to the *vendored, static 1.1.1w* --
while the libcurl.so.4 this same extension calls into is dynamically bound to
the system libssl.so.3. That is two OpenSSL implementations, with incompatible
SSL/SSL_CTX layouts (3.x made almost everything opaque), in one address space,
reachable from the same call. Without this patch, on 8.0, plain HTTP
curl_exec() works and HTTPS curl_exec() segfaults, reproducibly. php/ldflags.sh's --exclude-libs=ALL comment describes the same
collision from the other direction.

APPLIES TO 7.4 AND 8.0, AND ONLY THOSE

  7.4, 8.0  probe present, and these link the *system* libcurl while vendoring
            their own OpenSSL -- the mismatch. Patched, configure now answers
            "checking for libcurl linked against old openssl... no".
  7.0-7.3   the probe does not exist in their ext/curl/config.m4 at all.
  8.1-8.3   probe present, and it still answers *yes* -- measured, not assumed:
            their build logs say so, and HAVE_CURL_OLD_OPENSSL really is
            defined there. It is harmless because the modern era has no
            vendored OpenSSL, so PKG_CHECK_MODULES resolves to the very
            OpenSSL 3 that libcurl is already linked against. One OpenSSL, no
            collision. This is why 8.1-8.3 need no patch -- not because the
            probe passes there.
  8.4, 8.5  php-src removed the probe.

THE FIX

Treat "OpenSSL/3" the same as "OpenSSL/1.1": both have opaque structs and
neither needs the compat path. Not a blanket disable -- a genuine pre-1.1
OpenSSL, if one ever reappeared, still takes the old branch. This only closes
the version gap the 2020-era probe left open.

Not fixed upstream: both branches are EOL and will get no further releases.
The php-7.4 and php-8.0 copies of this patch are byte-identical (see
deps/patches/README.md); the probe region is unchanged between 7.4.33 and
8.0.30.

--- a/ext/curl/config.m4
+++ b/ext/curl/config.m4
@@ -39,7 +39,8 @@
     const char *ptr = data->ssl_version;

     while(*ptr == ' ') ++ptr;
-    if (strncasecmp(ptr, "OpenSSL/1.1", sizeof("OpenSSL/1.1")-1) == 0) {
+    if (strncasecmp(ptr, "OpenSSL/1.1", sizeof("OpenSSL/1.1")-1) == 0 ||
+        strncasecmp(ptr, "OpenSSL/3", sizeof("OpenSSL/3")-1) == 0) {
       /* New OpenSSL version */
       return 3;
     }
